  "notes": "Four-octave R&B-pop with a whistle-register ceiling. The Lane line names the straight class first — \"backbeat on 2&4 straight for the R&B/dance modes and half-time (accent on 3) for the trap-pop mode\" — so this model is `backbeat_24` with `halfTime` false and the trap-pop half-time stays documented rather than encoded. What survives from the trap-pop mode is its hat vocabulary: \"16th- and 32nd-note hat rolls\" is the roll `vocab` and a raised `freqPerBar`, and \"the R&B mode uses finger snaps and a swung, lightly ghosted backbeat\" is the `snap` lane plus a 16th `swing.amount` of 0.54 and a real ghost map. \"Percussion is sparse by design so the vocal stack has room\" pulls `percs.densityPerBar` and the perc gain down. The 808 is authored because the entry names it — \"heavy bass 808 sub with short glides is the thank u, next-era foundation … sub sits under, not against, the kick\" — as `counter_riff` so the synth/house bass keeps generating alongside it. Keys are the entry's own: \"D♭ major/minor is the single most recurrent tonal centre, then A♭ major and F minor\", plus C♯ minor on the named single. Harmony is \"4-bar minor-7th/9th vamps\", so `triad` is low. The melody block carries the documented topline: \"melisma-rich with fast descending runs, wide interval leaps into the upper register, and syllabic rapped hooks\", which is a high density, a descending-weighted contour, and the highest `octaveJumpProb` in this batch; the register ceiling is the whistle top used as an instrument. The counter is the \"dense stacked-harmony pad of her own voice\". ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are `swing.amount` 0.54 (\"swung, lightly ghosted\" is a direction, not a ratio), every drum probability, the humanize block and `bpm.mode` 122 (the research gives 63—150 with a working cluster 100—145, so the mode is that cluster's midpoint). ⚠ The catalogue tempo band carries the research's [LOW-CONF] mark.",
